<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-mahtmeatics-sampling-distributions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Mathematics of the Normal Distribution</title>
  <introduction>
    <p> 
      The previous section introduced the normal distribution and its
      importance to hypothesis testing. In particular, the need to
      calculate <m>z</m>-scores of sample statistics relative to a
      normal sampling distribution. In this section, I want to give the
      mathematics behind the <m>z</m>-score and some related ideas. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Recall that, for mean <m>\mu</m> and standard
      deviation <m>\sigma</m>, the normal distribution has this
      presentation as a mathematical function.
      <md>
        p(x) = \frac{1}{\sigma \sqrt{2\pi}}
        e^{\frac{-(x-\mu)^2}{2\sigma^2}} 
      </md>
      I'll use this mathematical function throught this section.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-root-pi">
    <title>The Square Root of <m>\pi</m></title>
    <p>
      Before getting too deep into the calculations that we need for
      hypothesis testing, I want to take a bit of a detour to talk
      about one very strange reality: why is there a square root of
      <m>\pi</m> in this definition? This is very odd. The number
      <m>\pi</m> is associated with circles and trigonometry. The bell
      curve is exponential decay. It doesn't seem to have anything to
      do with circles or trigonometry. Even if it did, the square root
      of <m>\pi</m> never shows up in trig at all. There aren't really
      any natural situations in circle geometry where taking the
      square root of <m>\pi</m> is a natural piece of arithmetic to
      do. This is really very odd.
    </p>
    <p>
      I'm going to give a partial explanation here, but not a full
      proof. Such a proof does exist and, if you are interesting, I
      usually do this proof in Math 315, the multi-variable calculus
      course. But for now, let me at least give you the setup. I'll
      return to the definition of a probability distribution. This was
      a function with three properties: it was never negative, it was
      defined on a range of values matching the probability outcomes,
      and the area under the curve was zero.
    </p>
    <p>
      When buliding mathematical functions to model probability, often
      we start with a known function and try to adopt it. I can take
      this approach to the bell curve. I'll start with the most basic
      verion of the function.
      <md>
        f(x) = e^{-x^2}
      </md>
      I choose this basic form for the double exponential decay that
      it provides. This is already the bell-curve shape, as show in
      <xref ref="figure-simple-bell"/>.
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="figure-simple-bell">
      <caption>Simple Bell Curve</caption>
      <image width="80%">
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}
          \begin{axis}[
              ytick=\empty,
            ]
            \addplot [
              domain=-3:3,
              samples=201,
            ]
            {exp(-(x)^2};
          \end{axis}
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      This function has a domain of all real numbers: I can put any
      number into this function, positive or negative, and get a
      result. This is fine for probability, even if in practice I only
      need a smaller range. This function is also always positive,
      since any exponential function will always output a positive
      number. The only issue with using this function as a probability
      distribution is the area. 
    </p>
    <p>
      So I need to calculate the  are under this function. In the
      notation and terminology I introduced in <xref
      ref="section-probability-theory"/>, I need to calculate an
      integral. 
      <md>
        \int_{-\infty}^\infty e^{-x^2} dx
      </md>
      I'm not going to calculate this integral today. This is a
      problematic integral even for a first-year calculus class, since
      none of the usual techniques help to solve it. However, I will
      give its value. 
      <md>
        \int_{-\infty}^\infty e^{-x^2} dx = \sart{2pi}
      </md>
      This is where the <m>\sqrt{2pi}</m> comes from: the techniques
      to calculate this integral end up using trigonomery in a clever
      way and produce this result. As for the detail of this mystery,
      the connection to trig, I have to leave that aside for this
      course. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Knowing the area under the curve, I can then scale the function
      by dividing by this area. 
      <md>
        p(x) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}} e^{-x^2}
      </md>
      This is now a probability distribution. It is positive, covers a
      probability range (more or less), and the area under the curve
      is now exactly <m>1</m>. 
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>f(x)</m> is a positive function defined on an interval
          <m>[a,b]</m> then let <m>M</m> be the area under the the
          graph of <m>f(x)</m> on its domain.  That is, <m>M</m> is
          the value of this integral.
          <md>
            \int_a^b f(x) dx - M
          </md>
          Then the <term>normalization</term> of the function is the
          probability distribution obtained by dividing by this
          constant. 
          <md>
            p(x) = \frac{1}{M} f(x) 
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      The word <sq>normalization</sq> is pretty heavily overused in
      mathematics. This is another use. The function here doesn't have
      to be a bell curve at all, so this use doesn't really have
      anything to do with the normal distribution. This is 
      frustrating, but is it standard terminology. 
    </p>
    <p>
      The integral and normalization explain the <m>\sqrt{2\pi}</m> in
      the normal distribution, as well as the division by the standard
      deviation in the general form. These divisions force the area
      under the distribution to be 1, which is necessary for the
      definition. 
    </p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-identifying">
    <title>Identifying Points</title>
    <p>
      For hypothesis testing, I need two things from the sampling
      distribution. First, I need to know its general shape. 
      Central Limit Theorem answers this, telling me that that
      sampling distribution will be a normal distribution, with a mean
      and a standard deviation calcluted from the sample data and the
      hypotheses. Second, I need to know where the sample statistic
      falls in the sampling distribution. I need to identify a point
      in the distribution.
    </p>
    <p>
      There are two ways to do identify a point in a probability
      distribution, and both have been mentioned
      already in previous sections. These are the <m>z</m>-score and
      the <m>p</m>-value. In this section, I'll go over some
      mathematical details of both. 
    </p>
    <p>
      I'll start with <m>z</m>-scores. As defined before, a
      <m>z</m>-score is a measure of how far a point is away from the
      mean, with the units of measurement being standard deviation. A
      <m>z</m>-score can be positibe or negative, since a point can be
      below or above the mean.
    </p>
    <p>
      The calculation of a <m>z</m>-score is, in fact, quite
      straightforward. If <m>x</m> is a point in the probability
      domain, the its <m>z</m>-score is given by this calculation.
      <md>
        z(x) = \frac{x-\mu}{\sigma}
      </md>
      The numerator here is the distance to the mean, again with
      positive or negative values indicating above or below the mean.
      Then I want this distance to use the units of standard
      deviation. Dividing by the standard deviation accomplishes this
      goal.  If I know the mean and standard deviation of a
      distribution, then calculating a <m>z</m>-score is just a bit of
      arithmetic. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Typically, in a hypothesis test, <m>z</m>-score is calculated
      first, since it is just this arithmetic. Then, the
      <m>z</m>-score can be use to calculate a <m>p</m>-value.
      Historically, this was done with pre-produced tables: you would
      take your <m>z</m>-score and look up thte matching
      <m>p</m>-value. However, since the advent of modern statistical
      software, we can now use more mathematically direct ways of
      calcluation. With that in mind, I'll move on <m>p</m>-values.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <m>p</m>-value is a probability. The sampling distribution
      is a probability distribution. This all can be presented in the
      mathematical language of probability distributions. In that
      language, area is probability. The probability of a value in
      some range is the area under the curve for that range. Consider
      the situation in <xref ref="figure-area-p-value"/>, where I am
      using a normal distribution with mean <m>6</m> and standard
      deviation <m>4</m>. 
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="figure-area-p-value">
      <caption>Areas and <m>p</m>-values</caption>
      <image width="80%">
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}
          \begin{axis}
              ytick=\empty,
            ]
            \addplot [
              domain=-6:22,
              samples=201,
            ]
            {exp(-(x-6)^2 / (2*4^2)) / (sqrt(2*pi*4^2))};
            \draw (15,0) -- (15,1);
          \end{axis}
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p> 
      In the figure, I've indicated a point at <m>x = 15</m>. I want
      to know the probability of that point or greater. Therefore, I
      am looking for area: specifically, the small area under the
      curve past the point <m>x=15</m>. That small area, as a
      proportion of 1, will be my <m>p</m>-value. In mathematics, area
      under curves is an integral. Therefore, this is the formal
      calculation for <m>p</m>-value of the sample statistic
      <m>a</m>, where <m>n(x)</m> is the normal sampling distribution.
      <md>
         p-\text{value} = \int_a^{\infty} n(x) dx
      </md>
      Formally, calculating <m>p</m>-value is calculating an integral.
      Moreover, it is an integral of the normal distribution, which
      even for calculus students is an inaccesible integral. This is
      why tables were important historically: this integral isn't
      accessible most of the time. For us, istead of tables we have
      software. R will calculate the integrals for us. The important
      point of this outcome is the connection of <m>p</m>-value to
      areas to integrals, conceptually. 
    </p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="sided-tests">
    <title>Sided Tests</title>
    <p>
      This is finally the place to discuss one of the subtleties of
      hypothesis testing that has lingered for a while now. When we
      made alternative hypotheses, we had a choice: do we take less
      than, greater than, or both (expressed as not equal). This
      choice affects the mathematics. 
    </p>
    <p>
      In the analysis about about <xref ref="figure-area-p-value"/>, I
      was looking specifically at the are above <m>x=14</m>. This only
      works for an alternative hypothesis that assume the sample
      statistic will be above the null hypothesis. This is the
      <sq>greater-than</sq> situation. What if the sample statistic is
      below the mean? In this situation, the figure looks more like
      <xref ref="figure-area-p-value2"/>/
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="figure-area-p-value2">
      <caption>Areas and <m>p</m>-values</caption>
      <image width="80%">
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}
          \begin{axis}
              ytick=\empty,
            ]
            \addplot [
              domain=-6:22,
              samples=201,
            ]
            {exp(-(x-6)^2 / (2*4^2)) / (sqrt(2*pi*4^2))};
            \draw (1,0) -- (1,1);
          \end{axis}
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      In this figure, the <m>p</m>-value will be the small area below
      the vertical line, not above, matching an alternative hypothesis
      that expects something below the mean, the <sq>less than</sq>
      situation. In this case, the formal calculation is also
      different. 
      <md>
         p-\text{value} = \int_{-\infty}^a n(x) dx
      </md>
      I'm measuing the area below the sample statistic, not above. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Finally, what if I had no idea about what to expect with the
      sample statistic, whether it was above or below? Well, then I'd
      consider both. In <xref ref="figure-area-p-value3"/>, I've drawn
      the line for the sample statistics as well as the matching line
      in mirror image on the other side of the distribution.
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="figure-area-p-value3">
      <caption>Areas and <m>p</m>-values</caption>
      <image width="80%">
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}
          \begin{axis}
              ytick=\empty,
            ]
            \addplot [
              domain=-6:22,
              samples=201,
            ]
            {exp(-(x-6)^2 / (2*4^2)) / (sqrt(2*pi*4^2))};
            \draw (-1,0) -- (-1,1);
            \draw (17,0) -- (17,1);
          \end{axis}
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      In this situation, I want the sum of the two outside areas,
      since I am consider the probability away from the mean on both
      sides. I can formalize all this in an important definition.
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          In hypothesis testing, there are two kinds of tests using
          the normal distribution.
          <ul>
            A <term>one-sided test</term> is a test that assumes the
            sample statistic must fall on one side of the mean. These
            are tests that use an alternative hypothesis with a
            one-directional inequality. They look at the area of one
            tail of the probability distrituion past the sample
            statistic, either on the left or on the right. 
          </ul>
          <ul>
            A <term>two-sided test</term> is a test that does not
            assume anything about which side of the mean to find the
            sample statistic. These are tests that use an alternative
            hypothesis with a simple negation of equality. They look
            at the area of both tails of the probability distribution
            outside of the sample statistic. 
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      When designing a research study, a researcher again has to make
      this choice. Is this a one-sided test or two-sided test? It
      comes back, again, to judgement. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Finally, one last important observation. The difference between
      one-sided tests and two-sided tests is whether or not to include
      the other tail. The normal distribution is symmetric: both tails
      have the same area. Therefore, the <m>p</m>-value for a
      two-sided test will be exactly twice the <m>p</m>-value for a
      one-sided test. In particularly, we can always just calculate
      the one-sided <m>p</m>-value and double it if we want a two
      sided test. 
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>
          
